#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1ca4d4-7351-58fd-97c2-4515882b9eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298095} :answer-field/id #uuid "1616ad67-70d1-5ebb-bb64-611f3e8271e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f95bba43-9c68-572a-b72d-21ed2ae6f555" :question/math-academy-id "q-318964" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,k\\,|=2\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $k$ and $l$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $k$ onto $l.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{l}\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{l}\\,k & =|\\,k\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74545791-26c1-5947-9c5b-b677dc493399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298054} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6c562c-1cf7-5f43-b2d5-435bef859ba2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "503c449a-b9d1-5796-9d9e-bf3065bb8dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-318971" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,r\\,|=10$ and the angle between vectors $r$ and $s$ is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =|\\,r\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10cos\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5684a7-6510-556f-b323-b27961d61e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297675} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac50c16-4972-5306-a766-6c8022e7162a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0d7d073-069e-5803-942e-5e5c5ad68481" :question/math-academy-id "q-318972" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=8$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39c7bca-ae4b-503b-9c4a-c72c47f4989e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298530} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d8a04d-078a-5212-83f2-c8e21cfb63c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3704c26f-1804-5b27-a680-7d8f815d3672" :question/math-academy-id "q-318975" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,t\\,|=\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $t$ and $u$ is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $t$ onto $u.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{u}\\,t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{u}\\,t & =|\\,t\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c94840-ea0a-5cb5-b3b6-df293a492727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297637} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d6fc074-46b1-5281-a18e-e811903a7f24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bbd01580-a282-5eb8-b608-20ac603d9781" :question/math-academy-id "q-318976" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,r\\,|=3\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $r$ and $s$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =|\\,r\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71e21be6-5288-5a39-b610-c229edf14988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298044} :answer-field/id #uuid "217237de-6b94-5d9f-a394-4d02f2866345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4084d86f-eb87-5f25-b405-7c5720bf45bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-318977" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨4,-1,1⟩$ and $q=⟨1,2,2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(1)+(-1)(2)+(1)(2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2+2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7883d86b-cdec-54e0-b22d-e3deedc52363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298077} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1742b44-a127-5c8f-93df-71d8fba4ab73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aaf8f1e8-3743-57db-8cf5-200275190fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-318979" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,-2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(2)+(-2)(1)+(1)(-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2-2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51311319-cccb-5499-b7df-82fada2209c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297893} :answer-field/id #uuid "32644fe5-7234-58cb-be30-d57ff4db1d29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7682317e-79ab-546a-b643-7edbcbe6f8ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-318981" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-1,2,3⟩$ and $s=⟨2,0,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{|\\,s\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+(2)(0)+(3)(0)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ecac01c-6f18-504a-a708-bcf3faabfed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297750} :answer-field/id #uuid "2737ed18-d118-51f4-b5e7-ebf40846ad60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5e680fa-ce4a-56db-a90a-7d183fdb5f25" :question/math-academy-id "q-318987" :question/problem "Let $m=⟨6,8,0⟩$ and $n=⟨0,5,12⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m-{\\text{comp}}_{m}\\,n.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m-{\\text{comp}}_{m}\\,n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m & =\\frac{m\\cdot n}{|\\,n\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{n}_{x}^{2}+{n}_{y}^{2}+{n}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)(0)+(8)(5)+(0)(12)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{169}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{m}\\,n & =\\frac{m\\cdot n}{|\\,m\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{m}_{x}^{2}+{m}_{y}^{2}+{m}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m-{\\text{comp}}_{m}\\,n & =\\frac{40}{13}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{40-52}{13} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c48ccf7-bca3-57a8-a2b5-7cdab2abc3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298234} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3473e6-0fcc-554b-8da2-28c6134ccc97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6dbc91c-6749-54fc-b502-867b88fba0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-318988" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨9,12,0⟩$ and $q=⟨0,3,4⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p-{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p-{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(9)(0)+(12)(3)+(0)(4)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p-{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{36}{5}-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95007d2-d2c4-5ca8-947f-ac698527e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298357} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8db6f26-99f0-50e3-8855-273485708ec8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "100a4e96-9db1-5d18-a7bd-1bd05f0073dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-319150" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,-1,2⟩$ and $q=⟨x,1,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 3x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0452a64-927f-5d7d-b07e-4b57abd4d6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298259} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b238f8d-e0d9-5ffc-aee7-5172fbc4d28f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "252df411-1410-5fbc-a0ba-6a3198b7a34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319171" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨4,1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨1,x,3⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc4d159-66a8-552d-8401-ac5170a3b165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297689} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b94070-fa19-5c0e-b7d6-fe0a4474788f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b9e2634-f76e-5353-8b9c-ac4ebc43947e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319172" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨-2,3,4⟩$ and $q=⟨1,2,x⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =-2+6+4x \\\\  & =4x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 4x+4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b09c2782-cc5c-5c6b-94f6-5ea932871845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27bc293-b846-5a95-9571-c426e151ab18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54bac378-b09e-5746-9da1-a5edf37aafcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-319252" :question/problem "Let $p=2i-3j+k$ and $q=xi+4j.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1b13d9-95f4-5e20-8b5e-53f42fb2123d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297784} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b7ba0f-63e5-5c5e-bfd2-b99c69925ef2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abcc12ab-195b-5a18-8e90-22ddbe9f6e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319253" :question/problem "Let $p=-4i+2j-3k$ and $q=5i+xj.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-20+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -20+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =20 \\\\ x & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8294dbd7-4cb6-51d9-8d20-d8495bf0ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298117} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e96eb66-c582-5560-9a9e-1bb66c30da90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7e28ce4-d965-5f90-abdd-fd4ebe18371a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319254" :question/problem "Let $p=3i+2j-k$ and $q=-2i+xj.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -6+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10667f31-4ce7-5455-9b8d-d46f3fda4165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297627} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0705119-20db-5fba-b904-b479530a6dc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c487f5aa-c96a-57d4-aea4-cb293f8d9612" :question/math-academy-id "q-319255" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90285a75-b811-5504-9cc1-612873c4e624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5e32c57-fed0-5cac-8815-45d1f36b47b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82fa0dd5-2719-5c00-bc10-1f3440618fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-319256" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ x \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3+5\\cdot x+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =5x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f12d002d-8b5a-5717-82a8-dcdfb665f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301151} :answer-field/id #uuid "521ede33-d2a8-59dd-89e4-28199ac9eddf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301497} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1a618a9-88f9-57c9-80c1-9fcf8f6645ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301155} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3dc363-42c5-5f38-9794-4c55f86ed7c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95e91ddf-2b12-5c46-a360-4d974b649d82" :question/math-academy-id "q-326267" :question/problem "![](images/34/3497daefa76dd730942b7deb1f32c42a30823c9de953f45ec011218aff126b21.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $9,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $J(2,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abe51396-4620-5389-8088-eb356477d2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300822} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b86c57-ae0e-5130-9a28-2409ed19074b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8a05776-5fce-5515-8294-ae821becc48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301038} :answer-field/id #uuid "bff13af6-00ce-5fe3-92f7-748fbc09ae53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34803c29-7d19-5aa5-ae0f-1a15ec82cbd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-327171" :question/problem "![](images/61/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a97e66-2a09-58d1-b194-8cbde87cf2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300964} :answer-field/id #uuid "416733ab-33f9-54eb-a0c5-5f41db87c0c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b43ca99b-795f-5c41-897b-d4a542a77d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300859} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfac471-9eb9-54e1-9348-066a3f546185" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f23032fb-7cf6-5197-9be4-ff4389fb406b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327172" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fcb18bca21a0469f03093edc82cbdec7fbfd410acd434c88a4086876f02d35c6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,1)$ with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,1).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2506dc2c-9a48-5ddf-a190-045af9bb3db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138} :answer-field/id #uuid "e51629cd-762c-5f50-ba6f-6feeb02fa23d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bcd20c00-207d-5776-ad99-e9ea0fea55dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-327798" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc28628-0500-5a4e-b7c4-aab08e1def7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b576c6-b6ea-547c-b701-c6162e1d6269" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf7a561b-81b3-5465-9ed7-22d55b60ba48" :question/math-academy-id "q-327803" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ec0879-40ef-5eca-bcab-024dcd2d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7318c6-8514-5dcc-b694-eb9615ec63ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42002e7f-0b06-57fa-9bb9-6d321bc37cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab159e0e-467d-5f21-8fde-88f54619f27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d87d77-faf2-51e3-8007-0a1b765c8eff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c43bad9-0cfd-52b5-8797-68675d788468" :question/math-academy-id "q-327809" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280} {:answer/id #uuid "611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327811" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63df4a4-ed4e-594d-a87c-7a4085320bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869} :answer-field/id #uuid "00924e31-27a5-58f1-944d-ca579e1c3713" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de50fd-2ab8-57fe-9cfb-ece3bde403ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300541} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a73176-b3ae-58c8-81f2-699e6e8ba975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7055cfca-1048-5afb-ba28-b8851f0876dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-327822" :question/problem "![](images/31/31b10310b585d867ecf883db2a5d778ec5ec9160df03fc4487d2ef7fd0b9a3af.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/73/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff88e02e-cfda-5128-8c1d-f91479a569f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227} :answer-field/id #uuid "5814c550-0d18-5b22-a1e2-06d2501b0824" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6680920-1294-53a9-9623-086e46da3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300952} :answer-field/id #uuid "c481516b-af38-5789-9db8-86dd24b8d0f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d17e2397-6da6-568e-b067-e70d0b979de7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327828" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f29c8db431035fda7e50771b3baaf6d19f51a60e26582957bbff0f70dbfdc5ed.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/24/24b56c8addea0d8a0f3effeeefc2788023c452e98b05d10db7893aeebde9ba5e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa59ec8d-cc80-5c21-9a98-1a321275e779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b1ece4-081f-5e29-9bb2-7e66dd6a50dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3ca0ddb-ad18-53c8-8fa4-f75ebc48934e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ef5771-df19-54b8-9c2c-99c1a01e2acc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50c33ba2-db10-58a1-9a90-99dd50ad5588" :question/math-academy-id "q-327831" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5bf161de62bc1b38aaeb87a327df551e8a7043f2867caa060d652cd065bece9.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171799d9-7223-5d